                    Highlights
                    1. Groundwater shows spatial variation in physicochemical and trace element levels.
                    2. Rock-water interaction and arid climate likely influence groundwater composition.
                    3. Contaminant transfer pathways are linked to lithology and land–water processes.
                    Keywords Arsenic contamination, Sulphides, Rock-water interaction, Groundwater
                    hydrogeochemistry, Geogenic sources, Tosham ring complex


                  1 Introduction
                  Water authorities are exploring effective and efficient mitigation strategies for access of
                  sustainable drinking water sources to meet increasing clean water demands for human
                  needs worldwide [1–3]. However, significant constraints exist that limit the access to
                  safe and clean drinking water, which consequently affect approximately 2.2 billion indi-
                  viduals globally [3, 4]. One of the contributing factors towards significant constraints
                  is widespread geogenic contaminants and emerging pollutants that are contributing to
                  the global depletion of clean water sources [5]. Consequently, the progress toward the
                  United Nations Sustainable Development Goal of ensuring access to clean water for
                  drinking and other human uses by 2030 is obstructing [3, 4].
                    Groundwater is a viable option for various household, industrial, and irrigation pur-
                  poses [5–7]. On a global scale, a large proportion of the population is dependent on
                  groundwater (e.g., ~ 50% for drinking, ~ 30% for agricultural purposes) [8, 9]. Given the
                  higher dependency on groundwater resources for different needs, the quantity and qual-
                  ity of groundwater aquifers changes [10–12]. In the context of global environment and
                  climate changes, the changes in groundwater quality represent a momentous issue [13].
                  Among the various groundwater quality concerns, the release of heavy metals has had a
                  significant impact on groundwater quality [14–16].
                    Arsenic has proven to be a serious threat for human health [17] and may be trans-
                  ported to soil, water, and air through various geogenic (e.g., geological formations, vol-
                  canic activities) and anthropogenic processes (e.g., mining activities) [18–21]. Arsenic
                  is present in both inorganic As(III) or As(V) species and organic As (e.g. monomethy-
                  larsonic acid, dimethylarsinic acid, trimethylarsine oxide, arsenobetaine, and arseno-
                  choline), while inorganic species are more toxic than organic compounds [22, 23]. The
                  occurrence of arsenic in aquifers is largely controlled by geological, soil chemical, hydro-
                  geological, and geomorphological factors [23–30]. The enrichment of As and its mobili-
                  zation in groundwater aquifers is controlled by three geochemical processes: adsorption,
                  reductive dissolution of Fe/Mn hydroxides, and oxidation of sulfide in arid and semi-arid
                  regions [28, 30, 34, 51, 57].
                    Geogenic contamination of As in groundwater has been reported worldwide. Accord-
                  ing to the WHO guidelines for drinking water quality, high As concentrations (> 0.01
                  ppm) in groundwater have affected 500 million people of more than 100 nations across
                  different continents [4, 31]. The most affected continents are Asia [32–34] and Europe
                  [26, 35], followed by Africa [36–39], North America [40], South America [20, 41, 42]
                  and Australia [43]. Note that groundwater quality deterioration has impacted human life
                  mainly in the villages of developing nations such as India [44].
                    In India, more than 70% of its population relies on groundwater for their drinking,
                  agriculture, and other needs [7]. However, the presence of various geogenic contami-
                  nants such as As, fluoride, Fe and nitrate restrict its safe usage. Arsenic and fluoride
Ram et al. Discover Geoscience   (2026) 4:108                                                                   Page 3 of 24




                  contaminated groundwater consumption almost inevitably influence more than 300 mil-
                  lion people in India [7, 45, 46]. Elevated As contamination in groundwater aquifers is
                  predominantly reported in alluvial deposits in the gangetic plain area, with a few excep-
                  tions observed in hard rock terrain of India [47, 48].
                     Arsenic contamination has been reported in aquifers of several states of India, includ-
                  ing Bihar, Jharkhand, Chhattisgarh, Manipur, Assam, Punjab, Haryana, and Uttar
                  Pradesh [7]. Populations in these regions are adversely affected by the consumption of
                  As-contaminated groundwater, leading to serious public health concerns [44, 49–56].
                  Studies suggest that nearly 50 million people are suffering from fatal diseases such as
                  cancer, arsenicosis, kidney damage, skin cancer and gallbladder cancer in these regions
                  which adversely impacts the socio-economic conditions of the country [28, 51, 57, 58].
                  Haryana, the state of India where the present research is being conducted, is also expe-
                  riencing significant As contamination in its groundwater resources. Five districts (Bhi-
                  wani, Fatehabad, Karnal, Rohtak, and Sonipat) have been repored to be affected by this
                  toxic geogenic contaminant [59, 60].
                     Although previous investigations [59] have reported elevated As concentrations in
                  selected locations, but the sampling is limited. While these findings provide preliminary
                  insights into the hydrochemical characteristics and potential exposure risks associated
                  with As-rich groundwater within the Tosham Ring Complex (TRC), some critical data
                  and knowledge gaps still exist. The aim of this study is to identify possible geochemical
                  controls on As enrichment in groundwater of the Tosham region, and the three main
                  objectives of this study are: (1) assessing the distribution of As contamination in ground-
                  water and soil; (2) investigating the influence of geogenic sources on As enrichment and
                  distribution in groundwater; and (3) evaluating the hydrogeochemical processes gov-
                  erning As release into groundwater. This study provides valuable information for pub-
                  lic health and groundwater management as it offers the critical insights for developing
                  effective As mitigation strategies in crystalline terrains like the Tosham Ring Complex
                  (TRC).

                  2 Study area
                  2.1 Overview and climate
                  The Tosham block is a sub district in the Bhiwani district of Haryana, India covering
                  an area of 745 Km2 (Fig. 1). According to the 2011 census, the block has 142,302 peo-
                  ple. The majority of the population is dependent on agriculture (wheat, mustard, cotton
                  and pearl millet). The climate of the block is characterized by arid to semi-arid condi-
                  tions. The area experiences an annual precipitation of approximately 300–450 mm, pre-
                  dominantly from the southwest monsoon, which is inconsistent and results in recurrent
                  drought-like conditions [59]. The temperature typically ranges from 3 to 45 °C. Daily and
                  seasonal ranges of temperature are high. Hot scorching, dust laden winds blow from the
                  Thar Desert of Rajasthan in June and July. The area is well known for its mining activities
                  of economic minerals for road metals and masonry stone. The main mining zones of the
                  blocks are the Khanak and Dadam villages.

                  2.2 Geology
                  The primary rock types found in Tosham consist of A-type granites, rhyolites, schists,
                  and quartzites of Precambrain age (Fig. 2). The Tosham hills are mainly composed of
Ram et al. Discover Geoscience        (2026) 4:108                                                                                      Page 4 of 24




                  Fig. 1 Location cum elevation map of the study area in Haryana, India, sample (both soil and groundwater) col-
                  lection sites




                  Fig. 2 Geological map of the study area, showing the spatial distribution of felsic volcanic lithologies associated
                  with the Tosham Ring Complex (TRC) 61

                  felsic and mafic volcanic rocks, and remains of Proterozoic magmatism [61]. The region
                  exhibits limited groundwater potential, attributable to its hard rock aquifers, where
                  groundwater is generally occur in fractured and weathered zones at depths of 30 to
                  100 m [59]. The crystalline rocks are exposed as ring dykes, ring formations, scattered
                  hummocks, remnant hills, and inselbergs. In certain areas, sand dunes conceal the rock
                  formations.
