                                 Hydrogeochemical process governing the aquifer
                                 By comparing the concentration of TDS vs (Na+ + K+)/(Na+ + K+ + Ca2+) and TDS versus Cl−∕ (Cl− + HCO3−)
                                 using Gibbs’ plot, the main hydrogeochemical processes occurring in the aquifers may be identified40. Three
                                 main categories may be found in Gibbs’ plot: evaporation dominance, rock, and precipitation. Plots like this one
                                 are often used to distinguish between different groundwater chemistry41. The following formulas were used to
                                 estimate the Gibbs’ ratios; all ionic measurements42 are in meq L−1.

                                                         Gibb’s ratio I = (Na+ + K+ )/(Na+ + K+ + Ca2+ )(4)

                                                                 Gibb’s ratio II = Cl− /(Cl− + HCO−
                                                                                                  3 )(5)


                                 The plots are the fraction of cations [(Na+ + K+)/(Na+ + K+ + Ca2+)] and anions [Cl−/(Cl− + HCO3−)] in contrast
                                 to TDS42.
                                    In the present study, the ratio of (Na+ + K+)/(Na+ + K+ + Ca2+) was less than 1 and ratio of Cl−/(Cl− + HCO3−)
                                 was less than 0.001. The Gibbs plot in Fig. 2a shows that the majority of samples were in the rock weathering


Scientific Reports |   (2024) 14:29843              | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-81379-x                                                  5
www.nature.com/scientificreports/


                                 dominate area. While rock-water interactions appear to be the primary process, the low Cl⁻ levels depicted in
                                 Fig. 2b suggest minimal influence from evaporative processes or marine sources. The plot shows a leftward shift
                                 in sample points, indicative of increased HCO3⁻ concentrations, implying that carbonate mineral dissolution is
                                 a significant process in the groundwater chemistry of Darrang. Factors contributing to HCO3⁻ enrichment in
                                 groundwater include the dissolution of carbonate minerals (e.g., calcite and dolomite) present in the region’s
                                 aquifer systems. Furthermore, the decomposition of organic matter in the soil increases CO2 levels, which, when
                                 dissolved in water, forms carbonic acid (H2CO3). This weak acid promotes the dissolution of carbonate minerals,
                                 releasing HCO3⁻ ions into the groundwater5. The district’s sedimentary formations, enriched with carbonate-
                                 bearing minerals and a mixture of younger and older alluvium, provide a continuous source of HCO3− through
                                 chemical weathering29. Moreover, the region’s high rainfall and humid subtropical climate promote infiltration
                                 and prolonged rock-water interactions, further facilitating mineral dissolution31. Various cross plots were drawn
                                 and presented (Fig. 2) in the study to gain an understanding of the specific hydrogeochemical processes at work
                                 in the research area.

                                 Delineating the major hydrogeochemical processes
                                 In the present study, EC (μS cm−1) analysis of groundwater revealed 61.6% were in good condition, while 36.8%
                                 were with slightly higher EC values. Evaporation typically results in higher concentrations of all species in
                                 water. If the evaporation process is dominant and no mineral species precipitate, then the Na+/Cl− ratio remains
                                 unchanged43, resulting in a horizontal line in Na+/Cl− vs EC, indicating concentration due to evaporation and
                                 evapo-transpiration. If Na is caused by halite breakdown, the Na+/Cl− molar ratio should be approximately equal
                                 to 1, while a ratio greater than 1 indicates Na released by a silicate weathering process44. In the present study, it
                                 was found that the molar ratio of Na+/Cl− in groundwater samples in the study area ranged from 219.7 to 29,465.
                                 As most of the samples in the study have Na+/Cl− molar ratio above 1, it indicates that silicate weathering is the
                                 major process in the study area45.
                                     The EC versus Na+/Cl− trend in groundwater suggests that evaporation is not a major geochemical process in
                                 the area. An increasing Na+:Cl− ratio with EC indicates an excess of Na+ from silicate weathering, which appears
                                 to be dominating process in the study (Fig. 3a). Groundwater samples from Nagaon (84%), Kamrup (49%), and
                                 West Karbi Anglong (78%)27, all revealed an excess of Na+ over Cl− including the study area Darrang (100%). The
                                 excess of Na+ compared to Cl− in the study area is caused by silicate weathering (Fig. 3b)46.
                                     The ratio of Na+ + K+ ions to total cations (Tz+) is used to determine the silicate weathering process5.
                                 Especially in hard-rock aquifers, silicate weathering is one of the primary geochemical processes regulating
                                 the main ions chemistry of the groundwater47. According to a previous study, samples from the Nagaon
                                 district, Assam exhibited silicate weathering since they were located along 0.5 Tz+ line (Na+ + K+) vs Total
                                 cation (Tz+) scatter diagram5. As per the findings of Stallard and Edmond48, samples that are situated above
                                 0.5 Tz+ aquiline are classified as low silicate weathering processes. In the present study (Fig. 3c), a portion of
                                 the samples lie around the 0.5 Tz+ line and trend towards Na+ + K+ = Tz+ line, indicating the impact of silicate




