                                     Calcite and dolomite are significant sources of Ca2+ and Mg2+, as indicated by plots close to the 1:1 line. If
                                 ion exchange is dominant, the points shift to the right, while in reverse ion exchange a contrasting pattern is
                                 observed resulting in an excess of Ca2+ and Mg2+ over49,50 SO42− and HCO3−. The scatter diagram (Ca2+ + Mg2+
                                 vs. HCO3− + SO42−) shows whether the ions originate from silicate or carbonate weathering. Figure 3e shows
                                 that all samples in the research area fell below the aquiline, showing that weathering of silicate minerals is the
                                 predominant hydrochemistry of groundwater that supplies ions in solution, with ion exchange dominating for
                                 points shifted to the right. Most samples in Ca2+ versus HCO3− ratio plot tend to be above 1:2 (Fig. 3f), indicating
                                 calcite weathering. Only 0.58% (n = 2) of samples fell below the 1:1 line, indicating carbonate weathering8.
                                     A Ca2+ Vs SO42− plot that is closer to the 1:1 line indicates that the source of Ca2+ and SO42− in groundwater
                                 is gypsum dissolution51. Most of the samples lie below the 1:1 equiline, as seen in Fig. 3g. This groundwater’s
                                 excess of Ca2+ over SO42− suggests that Ca2+ is derived by gypsum dissolution51. Ca2+ + Mg2+ concentrations
                                 in groundwater were much higher than Cl− (Fig. 3h), indicating that Ca2+ + Mg2+ surpasses salinity at a higher
                                 rate45.

                                 Arsenic mobilization
                                 The spatial distribution map for TDS, As, Fe and SO42− is shown in Fig. 4. It is evident that sites with elevated
                                 TDS (Fig. 4a) are higher in As levels. The maps (Fig. 4b–d) show a comprehensive picture of the prevalence and
                                 distribution of As, Fe and SO42− concentrations across the district.
                                     In this study, high As groundwater (> 10 μg L−1) typically had pH values of 6–8 or higher (Fig. 5a). An
                                 increase in As levels has been linked to elevated HCO3− levels in reported studies5. According to Kim et al.52,
                                 higher HCO3− can cause As to be released into groundwater, indicating that the groundwater recharge process
                                 speeds up As leaching in aquifers. From Fig. 5b, the HCO3− reveals a poor correlation with As concentration




