                                 (R2 = 0.0562), suggesting that HCO3− has a direct competitive effect on As for the adsorption site is not the only
                                 dominant process involved in Darrang’s As mobilization53. The possibility that the mobilization of As in ground
                                 water is caused by the reduction of Fe oxide is suggested by a weak positive correlation (R2 = 0.0035) noticed
                                 between As and Fe (Fig. 5c). Moreover, groundwater pH was found to be alkaline with > 50% of the samples
                                 showing pH within 7.5–8.5 with mean pH 7.4. Therefore, desorption of As in alkaline environment may be one
                                 of the significant As release mechanism in the region. The observed high As concentrations can be explained
                                 by the desorption of As from Fe (hydr)oxides during aquifer recharge. Similar observations were found in the
                                 upper Brahmaputra plain, reporting As enriched groundwater in high pH environment facilitating dissolution
                                 of arsenic from minerals9.
                                     The oxidation of As-containing SO42− releases As in oxidizing aquifer conditions10. Thus, when it comes to
                                 oxidizing groundwater, As and SO42− have a beneficial relationship. In the present study, it is evident from the
                                 low concentrations of SO42− in groundwater samples and the weak correlation (R2 = 0.0073) between As and
                                 SO42− (Fig. 5d) that As has not been directly mobilized from sulfide minerals such as arsenopyrite54. The lack
                                 of significant association in this study may be because of the aquifers’ declining environmental conditions8. The
                                 SO42− geographical distribution indicates that the study’s total concentration is far below the WHO37 (< 250 mg
                                 L−1) recommended daily intake levels.
                                     Figure 6 illustrates the processes involved in the mobilization of As in groundwater and its transport to
                                 drinking water, emphasizing the associated health risks, bioaccumulation, and transfer mechanisms. The
                                 ingestion of arsenic-contaminated water poses severe health risks, including cancer, skin lesions, cardiovascular
                                 diseases, and neurological effects. Bioaccumulation occurs as arsenic accumulates in aquatic life, plants, and




