www.nature.com/scientificreports/


                                 livestock, entering the food chain and posing additional risks through the consumption of contaminated
                                 food and water. This comprehensive pathway highlights the importance of monitoring and mitigating arsenic
                                 contamination to protect human health.

                                 Hierarchical cluster analysis
                                 Hierarchical Cluster analysis (HCA) assists in predicting the common qualities shared by several variables.
                                 HCA was used in the study to understand the complex hydro geochemistry resulting from several processes
                                 namely ion exchange, and weathering of anthropogenic or natural origin9. From Fig. 7, it is evident that two
                                 clusters were generated which represents the hydro-chemical processes of the region. Cluster 1 comprised of
                                 Ca2+, Mg2+, EC, TA, HCO3−, K+, pH and Cl− whereas cluster 2 comprised of SO42−, Fe, As, F and Na+ (Fig. 7).
                                 Cluster 1 indicates the occurrence of pH induced carbonate weathering process implying active dissolution as a
                                 governing mechanism. Cluster 2 suggests influence of anthropogenic activities such as use of chemical fertilizers
                                 during cultivation, as well as geogenic process such as weathering of minerals is increasing the concentration of
                                 associated contaminants in the groundwater55,56. Overall, the HCA of the water quality parameters suggest the
                                 influence of local geology and agricultural practices contributing to the presence of the contaminants.

