Heavy metals in vegetables:
screening health risks involved in cultivation
along wastewater drain and irrigating
with wastewater
Ashita Sharma, Jatinder Kaur Katnoria and Avinash Kaur Nagpal*
*Correspondence:
avnagpal@yahoo.co.in
Department of Botanical
and Environmental Sciences,
Guru Nanak Dev University,
Amritsar, Punjab 143005,
India

Abstract
Irrigation of agricultural land with wastewater leads to continuous buildup of metals
at these sites which gets accumulated in the vegetables and crops growing on these
sites. Not just the crops irrigated with wastewater are hazardous, in present study, we
have found that vegetables growing in vicinity of wastewater drain are also not safe
for human consumption. The risk associated with consumption of vegetables was
assessed by calculating hazard quotient and results revealed that the hazard quotient for leafy and tuberous vegetables was higher than the safe limits in all the sites
irrespective of mode of irrigation. Spinach was the most hazardous among all as the
hazard quotient with respect to cobalt and copper was highest in spinach. Uptake
trend of metals in all vegetables: Iron > Cobalt > Copper > Cadmium > Lead. Cadmium,
a potential carcinogen was found in concentrations higher than permissible limits in
many vegetables from all sites. Highest level of cadmium (1.20 mg/kg) and copper
(81.33 mg/kg) was reported in site which was in vicinity of waste water drain but irrigated with ground water. Concentration of copper and lead in vegetable samples from
different sites exhibited no statistically significant difference with respect to different
sites.
Keywords: Heavy metals, Hazard quotient, Metal pollution index, Vegetables,
Wastewater

Background
Injudicious use of resources and haphazard urbanization have over-exploited the natural resources and caused detrimental effects on the environment. Unplanned economic
development has led to pressure on cultivable land and suitable water for irrigation. To
meet the food demands of exponentially growing human population, cultivation of food
crops is carried out at places which are not suitable for agriculture like along wastewater
drains or other polluted sites. To address water crisis, irrigation using the large amount
of wastewater discharged from the rapid growing industries is being carried out in
many parts of the world. Wastewater used for irrigation has many contaminants mainly
heavy metals depending upon the source of discharge (Huibers and Van Lier 2005;
Pedrero et al. 2010). In most of the developing countries, the wastewater discarded from
